WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.292 --> 00:00:09.464
I would love to introduce you
to three remarkable women I know.

00:00:10.382 --> 00:00:16.680
Meet Jane Maigua,
Charity Wangui and Loise Maina.

00:00:17.347 --> 00:00:20.850
Now, these three women
are restoration champions.

00:00:20.892 --> 00:00:26.940
They started a company six years ago
in my hometown of Nairobi,

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:29.401
purchasing macadamia nuts from farmers,

00:00:29.442 --> 00:00:33.446
processing them and selling them
around the world.

00:00:34.406 --> 00:00:40.662
Today, Exotic EPZ is in partnership
with 9,000 farmers across the country.

00:00:41.371 --> 00:00:45.125
Imagine for a minute
the tree-growing movement

00:00:45.166 --> 00:00:48.628
these women could catalyze across Kenya.

00:00:49.296 --> 00:00:52.549
Now here's another group
of women that I absolutely adore.

00:00:52.591 --> 00:00:55.302
These are the women of Maragua.

00:00:56.261 --> 00:01:01.891
These women are discovering
the wonders of bamboo.

00:01:02.851 --> 00:01:09.274
They are planting bamboo and sharing
across their communities.

00:01:09.774 --> 00:01:12.319
They're using bamboo for entrepreneurship.

00:01:12.652 --> 00:01:19.117
They're using bamboo for restoration,
and they're using it for firewood.

00:01:20.076 --> 00:01:24.831
Now these restoration champions
have something in common.

00:01:25.332 --> 00:01:30.045
They are using nature to reduce poverty,

00:01:30.045 --> 00:01:34.633
accelerate the re-greening of Africa

00:01:34.633 --> 00:01:37.427
and secure livelihoods.

00:01:38.053 --> 00:01:41.973
Now, this is part of the AFR100 Movement

00:01:42.015 --> 00:01:47.729
an Africa-wide initiative to restore
100 million hectares of land

00:01:47.729 --> 00:01:49.230
by 2030.

00:01:50.357 --> 00:01:56.112
All on a continent where we know
60 percent of soils are degraded.

00:01:57.155 --> 00:01:59.074
And this is only the beginning

00:01:59.115 --> 00:02:02.452
because the restoration
potential in Africa

00:02:02.494 --> 00:02:05.747
is 750 million hectares,

00:02:05.789 --> 00:02:09.501
equal to the size landmass of Australia.

00:02:11.252 --> 00:02:16.841
And this on a continent
where 60 percent of the land is degraded.

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:19.594
Now, why is this important?

00:02:19.636 --> 00:02:25.308
This is because restoration
breaks the cycle of degradation.

00:02:25.308 --> 00:02:28.853
It is at once about climate mitigation

00:02:28.895 --> 00:02:34.234
and cushioning communities against
the worst impacts of climate change.

00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:37.570
My organization, the World
Resources Institute,

00:02:37.612 --> 00:02:40.782
is working with AFR100 very closely.

00:02:41.157 --> 00:02:46.246
We are helping them to raise resources
needed for restoration,

00:02:46.246 --> 00:02:50.083
to accelerate the establishment
of sound policies

00:02:50.125 --> 00:02:53.336
so that restoration is incentivized.

00:02:53.336 --> 00:02:56.131
And also establishing
platforms like this one,

00:02:56.172 --> 00:03:00.760
so restoration champions
have the platforms and there’s training

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:04.222
that they need to accelerate restoration.

00:03:06.433 --> 00:03:10.186
Now without these local leaders,

00:03:10.186 --> 00:03:12.856
local wisdom, local knowledge

00:03:12.897 --> 00:03:16.526
and the passion that drives
restoration champions,

00:03:16.568 --> 00:03:19.404
scaling restoration
on the African continent

00:03:19.404 --> 00:03:22.115
would virtually be impossible.

00:03:23.241 --> 00:03:27.787
Now, my name is Wanjira Mathai,
and I have a personal connection to trees.

00:03:28.788 --> 00:03:31.124
When we were growing up,
my brothers and I,

00:03:31.166 --> 00:03:32.584
our mother, Wangari Mathai,

00:03:32.584 --> 00:03:35.003
who founded the Green Belt Movement

00:03:35.044 --> 00:03:38.590
and won the 2004 Nobel
Peace Prize for this work --

00:03:38.631 --> 00:03:39.799
(Applause)

00:03:39.799 --> 00:03:40.967
Thank you.

00:03:41.009 --> 00:03:42.927
(Applause)

00:03:42.969 --> 00:03:44.971
She spent hours

00:03:44.971 --> 00:03:50.518
sharing just how beautiful Kenya was
when she was growing up.

00:03:51.394 --> 00:03:54.147
The river’s, crystal clear.

00:03:55.023 --> 00:03:58.735
The hillsides and valleys,
covered in forests.

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:03.156
The plains were teeming with wildlife
and the soils, she would say,

00:04:03.156 --> 00:04:07.202
produced the most delicious
sweet potatoes and vegetables.

00:04:07.535 --> 00:04:10.038
Hunger was virtually unknown.

00:04:11.122 --> 00:04:14.709
By the 1970s, a lot had changed.

00:04:15.752 --> 00:04:21.508
Landscapes were already degraded,
rivers were silted with precious topsoil.

00:04:22.175 --> 00:04:26.054
The plains were already
losing their wildlife,

00:04:26.095 --> 00:04:29.516
and the soils were largely degraded.

00:04:29.849 --> 00:04:33.186
Now, half a century on,

00:04:33.186 --> 00:04:37.398
and the process of degradation
has only accelerated.

00:04:38.024 --> 00:04:39.943
And not only in Kenya.

00:04:40.276 --> 00:04:44.989
27 of the 40 most climate-vulnerable
countries in the world

00:04:44.989 --> 00:04:47.408
are found on the African continent.

00:04:47.867 --> 00:04:49.994
And hunger and drought,

00:04:49.994 --> 00:04:55.333
for those farmers, 60 percent of them
who live on the African continent,

00:04:55.375 --> 00:04:58.378
is an everyday occurrence.

00:04:58.419 --> 00:05:00.255
But why is this important?

00:05:00.296 --> 00:05:02.215
Why is this important?

00:05:02.257 --> 00:05:09.013
Because restoration reverses
and breaks the cycle of degradation.

00:05:09.055 --> 00:05:10.598
And there is hope.

00:05:11.015 --> 00:05:12.559
Because here is an example,

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:13.977
the Green Belt Movement.

00:05:14.352 --> 00:05:18.898
The Green Belt Movement is an example
of a restoration champion in Kenya.

00:05:19.774 --> 00:05:26.531
50 million trees planted
by a network of 5,000 women today.

00:05:26.990 --> 00:05:31.327
And they do this by organizing
themselves into groups,

00:05:31.327 --> 00:05:36.499
generating tree nurseries,
collecting seeds themselves that they sow,

00:05:36.499 --> 00:05:40.962
generate seedlings and plant
on public land and private land.

00:05:41.838 --> 00:05:45.592
This is the genius
of restoration champions.

00:05:45.925 --> 00:05:51.472
Now, I have seen this work firsthand
in a forest not too far from where I live,

00:05:51.514 --> 00:05:53.224
the Aberdare Forest.

00:05:53.224 --> 00:05:55.977
This is one of my favorite forests.

00:05:56.019 --> 00:05:59.230
A few years ago it was degraded,

00:05:59.230 --> 00:06:02.692
but thanks to the pioneering work
of the Green Belt Movement

00:06:02.692 --> 00:06:05.153
and many others like them,

00:06:05.153 --> 00:06:08.364
this forest has largely been restored.

00:06:08.406 --> 00:06:12.535
And I love spending time in this forest

00:06:12.577 --> 00:06:16.164
because as far
as the eye can see are trees.

00:06:16.164 --> 00:06:22.378
You could almost see them drawing out
carbon dioxide and pumping out oxygen,

00:06:22.420 --> 00:06:24.839
holding on to precious topsoil.

00:06:25.757 --> 00:06:28.509
And the crystal clear waterfalls

00:06:28.509 --> 00:06:32.180
that supply the rivers of Nairobi,

00:06:32.180 --> 00:06:34.474
the city where I live.

00:06:36.267 --> 00:06:42.649
AFR100 will take practices like these
across the African continent

00:06:42.649 --> 00:06:44.651
and replicate them

00:06:44.651 --> 00:06:48.196
not merely to halt deforestation,

00:06:48.196 --> 00:06:49.864
but to reverse it.

00:06:50.657 --> 00:06:53.409
Because that's what is needed.

00:06:54.118 --> 00:06:57.330
Now, it's not uncommon for people to say

00:06:57.330 --> 00:07:00.792
that initiatives like those
supported by AFR100

00:07:00.792 --> 00:07:05.505
are too small or too risky
for meaningful investments.

00:07:06.130 --> 00:07:08.925
But our work at AFR100 shows

00:07:08.925 --> 00:07:15.348
that not only are these initiatives
economical and transformative,

00:07:15.348 --> 00:07:17.892
they make financial sense.

00:07:18.851 --> 00:07:22.522
Our research also shows
that initiatives like these

00:07:22.563 --> 00:07:24.691
that are locally led and managed

00:07:24.691 --> 00:07:30.196
are 20 times more likely
to deliver long-term success.

00:07:30.989 --> 00:07:37.286
And they are also more likely to deliver
economic and environmental benefits.

00:07:38.162 --> 00:07:43.376
So if we invest in initiatives
like Tilaa in Ghana,

00:07:43.376 --> 00:07:45.670
AFR100,

00:07:45.712 --> 00:07:47.171
the Green Belt movement,

00:07:47.213 --> 00:07:51.217
GreenPort in Kenya and ARCOS in Rwanda,

00:07:51.259 --> 00:07:57.140
they will in turn invest in thousands
and thousands of restoration champions

00:07:57.181 --> 00:07:59.308
who will restore their landscapes

00:07:59.308 --> 00:08:01.811
and restore their livelihoods.

00:08:02.186 --> 00:08:08.109
Now, this is exactly the sort of inclusive
transformation we need to see

00:08:08.109 --> 00:08:10.653
across the African continent.

00:08:11.821 --> 00:08:13.948
Now all my life,

00:08:13.948 --> 00:08:16.993
I was made acutely aware

00:08:17.035 --> 00:08:22.415
of just how precious
green vegetation truly is.

00:08:23.708 --> 00:08:27.503
Nature is the source of everything good

00:08:27.545 --> 00:08:29.839
my mother would tell me all the time.

00:08:31.049 --> 00:08:35.261
You see, this vision we have
to re-green the African continent,

00:08:35.261 --> 00:08:37.597
it's not only possible,

00:08:37.597 --> 00:08:39.807
it is vital.

00:08:40.558 --> 00:08:42.727
It is our life support system.

00:08:43.853 --> 00:08:47.398
Now, many of us here despair
that we are the generation

00:08:47.398 --> 00:08:49.233
that is destroying the planet.

00:08:49.650 --> 00:08:51.319
But we don't have to be.

00:08:51.652 --> 00:08:55.615
We can be the restoration generation.

00:08:55.948 --> 00:08:57.158
Thank you.

00:08:57.158 --> 00:08:59.619
(Applause)

